    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Births New Adventure Scooter Family, Three RW Models That Look More Like Motorcycles\nMcLaren's New McL 820 Supercar To Offer HDK Version, Reportedly Weighs 1,550 Kilos\n45 photos\nPhoto: McLaren\nWhat McLaren calls McL 820 is a brand-new supercar due in the first half of 2027, potentially for the 2028 model year in the United States market. Per fresh information obtained by The Supercar Blog, this fellow is coming in two distinct versions, packs a heavily revised twin-turbo V6 derived from that of the poor-selling Artura, and weighs just around 1,550 kilograms (3,417 pounds).\nInternally referred to as P34, the newcomer presumably succeeds the Artura rather than both the plug-in hybrid supercar and the internal combustion-only 7xx series. McLaren revealed the McL 820 moniker in October 2026, and in typical Macca fashion, the numerical side of the name indicates maximum horsepower.\nMetric horsepower, that is, with 820 ps converting to 809 mechanical ones. That figure alone puts the McL 820 above the revised Artura and the punchiest member of the 7xx family, but alas, the rumored curb weight makes it heavier than the model it replaces. Weirder still, the Artura could remain in production until 2028.\nThe Supercar Blog also reports that McLaren will retain the Artura's twin-turbo V6, albeit with many revisions. One source described the McL 820's engine as nearly new, a selection of words that clearly indicates more than software calibration and less restrictive cats for the exhaust system.\nInstead of a 60- or 90-degree layout, the M630 in the Artura is a wide-angle affair that prides itself on 120 degrees. Only the Prancing Horse of Maranello produces a similar engine as of this writing, and both automakers decided on spreading the banks outward this much to lower the center of gravity.\nPhoto: McLaren A wide-angle sixer like the M630 is great in terms of packaging as well, delivering structural advantages in the process. Better still, that 120-degree V allows opposing cylinders to share one crankpin on a stiff three-throw crankshaft, enabling an 8,500-redline. On its own, this engine develops close to 600 ponies and 431 pound-feet (585 Newton-meters) of twist.\nTogether with the high-voltage battery powering the axial-flux electric drive unit, make that 690 horsepower and 531 pound-feet (720 Newton-meters). Pretty serious maximum output figures, but alas, the Artura has always disappointed in other areas. Not only does it sound a bit uninspiring, but its styling is best described as clinical when compared to that of the 296.\nRegarding the purported high-downforce package, McLaren produced four such kits in the past three decades or so. It all started with the three-seater F1 in the 1990s, followed by the MSO HDK flavor of the Mercedes-Benz SLR McLaren. Then came the 570S MSO High Downforce Kit, followed by the 750S Le Mans special edition from 2025.\nCelebrating the 30th anniversary of the F1 GTR's 1995 overall victory at the 24 Hours of Le Mans, the Le Mans delivers 10 percent more downforce than a standard 750S. From the raised carbon-fiber active rear spoiler to the revised front bumper with a larger carbon-fiber splitter and the louvered under-wing panel, it's easy to understand how McLaren extracted more downforce from the incredibly capable 750S.\nThe cited publication hasn't detailed the McL 820 HDK at all, but we can easily imagine that McLaren will lean on the lessons learned from the 750S Le Mans for this upcoming high-downforce kit.\nVia // The Supercar Blog\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMcLaren McL 820 McLaren Artura McLaren V6 rumors\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Spacecraft Reaches Asteroid NASA Hit Hard in 2022 for the First-Ever Space Crash Site Investigation",
